ubtype":76,"geo":17},"Reportedly found near one of the graves in the necropolis of Contrada Rifriscolaro, together with other fragments. The vessel formed part of a seizure carried out by the Carabinieri in 1974 and lacks a secure archaeological context.","found","discovered",[],{"_":79,"type":80,"subtype":81,"geo":17},"None","observed","autopsied",[],"Sicily","Ragusa",{"_":86,"role":87,"ref":88,"museum":89},"Museo Archeologico Ibleo","museum","http://sicily.classics.ox.ac.uk/museum/091",{"slug":90,"type":87,"name":86,"uri":88,"description":91,"location":92},"091","Comprehensive collection of archaeological material discovered in the province of Ragusa, with a particular focus on the sites of Kaukana, Kamarina, Castiglione and Monte Casasia",{"settlement":84,"region":84,"country":93,"address":94,"geo":95},"Italy","Via Natalelli, 97100 Ragusa (RG)",{"lat":96,"lon":97},36.9239,14.72727,{"_":99,"mainLang":100,"languages":101},"Ancient Greek","grc",[99],{"type":103,"bibl":104},"edition",[105],{"author":106,"date":107,"citedRange":108,"ptr":109,"ref":111,"inscriptionDate":107,"title":113,"citation":114,"uri":115,"year":107},"Scerra","2018","472-473, figs. 7-8",{"target":110},"http://zotero.org/groups/382445/items/BKW9Q5A5",{"_":112,"target":112},"https://biblio.inscriptiones.org/epig10002691","Le necropoli di età greca dall'Irminio a Camarina : vecchi dati, recenti acquisizioni e nuove considerazioni su popolamento, commerci e culti","\u003Cspan>Saverio Scerra, «Le necropoli di età greca dall’Irminio a Camarina : vecchi dati, recenti acquisizioni e nuove considerazioni su popolamento, commerci e culti», in \u003Ci>Ollus leto datus est : architettura, topografia e rituali funerari nelle necropoli delli Italia meridionale e delle Sicilia tra antichità e medioevo : atti del convegno internazionale di studi Reggio Calabria, 22-25 ottobre 2013 1: Dalla preistoria all’ellenismo\u003C/i>, a c. di Carmela Malacrino e Simonetta Bonomi (Reggio di Calabria: Museo Archeologico Nazionale di Reggio Calabria, 2018), 465–74\u003C/span>","https://www.zotero.org/groups/isicily/items/BKW9Q5A5",{"type":117,"bibl":118},"discussion",[17],{"editor":120,"principal":123,"contributors":126,"change":141},{"_":121,"ref":122},"Jonathan Prag","#JP",{"_":124,"ref":125},"Valentina Mignosa","#VM",[127,130,134,138],{"_":121,"xml:id":128,"ref":129},"JP","http://orcid.org/0000-0003-3819-8537",{"_":131,"xml:id":132,"ref":133},"James Chartrand","JCh","http://orcid.org/0000-0001-6823-0265",{"_":135,"xml:id":136,"ref":137},"Simona Stoyanova","SS","https://orcid.org/0000-0003-3914-9569",{"_":124,"xml:id":139,"ref":140},"VM","https://orcid.org/0000-0002-7122-2511",{"_":142,"when":143,"who":125},"Creation of file and EpiDoc encoding","2026-07-31","",[],[147],"Scerra, 2018, Le necropoli di età greca dall'Irminio a Camarina : vecchi dati, recenti acquisizioni e nuove considerazioni su popolamento, commerci e culti",[149],"BKW9Q5A5",[151,152,153,19,23,27,154,154,155,156,157,158,100],"isic020939","graffito naming hera",{"_":5},"kamarina","sicily","ragusa","museo archeologico ibleo","ancient greek",[],{"title":161,"body":162,"divs":163,"editions":188,"images":198,"handnote":199},"ISic020939. Graffito naming Hera","\u003Cdiv id=\"editions\">\u003Cdiv class=\"attribution\">Text encoding: Valentina Mignosa\u003C/div>\u003Cdiv id=\"edition-interpretive\" lang=\"grc\">  \u003Cdiv class=\"textpart\" lang=\"grc\">\u003Cspan class=\"ab\" id=\"div-interpretive\">  \u003Ca id=\"al1-interpretive\">\u003C!--0-->\u003C/a>\u003Cspan class=\"linenumber\">1\u003C/span>\u003Cspan data-type=\"name\" data-name-type=\"\" data-text=\"Ἥρα\">Ἥρα\u003C/span>  \u003C/span>\u003C/div>  \u003Cp class=\"miniapp\">\u003C/p>\u003C/div>\u003Cdiv class=\"attribution\">Text encoding: Valentina Mignosa\u003C/div>\u003Cdiv id=\"edition-diplomatic\" lang=\"grc\">\u003Cdiv class=\"textpart\" lang=\"grc\">\u003Cspan class=\"ab\" id=\"div-diplomatic\">\u003Ca id=\"al1-diplomatic\">\u003C!--0-->\u003C/a>\u003Cspan class=\"linenumber\">1\u003C/span>\u003Cspan data-type=\"name\" data-name-type=\"\" data-text=\"Ἥρα\">ΗΡΑ\u003C/span>\u003C/span>\u003C/div>\u003Cp class=\"miniapp\">\u003C/p>\u003C/div>\u003C/div>  \u003Cdiv id=\"facsimile-images\">\u003Ch2>Facsimile Images\u003C/h2>\u003C/div>  \u003Cdiv id=\"apparatus\">\u003Ch4>Apparatus criticus\u003C/h4>\u003Cul>\u003Cli>\u003Cspan>\u003Cspan>Text after photograph\u003C/span>\u003C/span>\u003C/li>\u003C/ul>\u003C/div>  \u003Cdiv id=\"translation\">\u003Ch2>English translation\u003C/h2>\u003Cp class=\"translation-author\">\u003Ca href=\"https://orcid.org/0000-0002-7122-2511\">Valentina Mignosa\u003C/a>\u003C/p>  \u003Cp>(To?) Hera\u003C/p>  \u003C/div>  \u003Cdiv id=\"commentary\">\u003Ch2>commentary\u003C/h2>  \u003Cp>The complete name of Hera is scratched on the exterior of the rim of a fragmentary black-glaze patera. Scerra regards the graffito as one of the few secure pieces of evidence for cult practice at Kamarina and notes that no earlier evidence for the worship of Hera had been identified in the city. Comparable vase graffiti giving the complete name of the goddess are known from Gela. The vessel type was current from the last quarter of the fifth century through much of the fourth century BCE, but the absence of a secure archaeological context prevents a more precise dating. However, the orthography of the inscription—with open long /ɛː/ represented by epsilon and aspiration by het—would favour an earlier date, between the mid-sixth and early fifth century BCE. Scerra considers that the graffito could record a devotional act performed by a Geloan settler after the refoundation of Kamarina in 461 BCE, while also suggesting that the cult may have reached the city earlier. The inscription supports the presence of devotion to Hera but does not by itself demonstrate the existence of a formal sanctuary. \u003C/p>  \u003C/div>  \u003Cdiv id=\"bibliography\">\u003Ch2>bibliography\u003C/h2>  \u003Cul>\u003Cli> Scerra 2018 472-473, figs. 7-8 \u003Ca href=\"https://biblio.inscriptiones.org/epig10002691\" target=\"_blank\">https://biblio.inscriptiones.org/epig10002691\u003C/a>  \u003C/li>\u003C/ul>  \u003Cul>\u003Cli>\u003C/li>\u003C/ul>  \u003C/div>  \u003Cdiv id=\"handnote\"> Line 1    \u003C/div>\u003Cdiv id=\"license\"> Licensed under a Creative Commons-Attribution 4.0 licence. \u003C/div>",[164,167,170,173,176,179,182,185],{"id":165,"html":166},"editions","\u003Cdiv class=\"attribution\">Text encoding: Valentina Mignosa\u003C/div>\u003Cdiv id=\"edition-interpretive\" lang=\"grc\">  \u003Cdiv class=\"textpart\" lang=\"grc\">\u003Cspan class=\"ab\" id=\"div-interpretive\">  \u003Ca id=\"al1-interpretive\">\u003C!--0-->\u003C/a>\u003Cspan class=\"linenumber\">1\u003C/span>\u003Cspan data-type=\"name\" data-name-type=\"\" data-text=\"Ἥρα\">Ἥρα\u003C/span>  \u003C/span>\u003C/div>  \u003Cp class=\"miniapp\">\u003C/p>\u003C/div>\u003Cdiv class=\"attribution\">Text encoding: Valentina Mignosa\u003C/div>\u003Cdiv id=\"edition-diplomatic\" lang=\"grc\">\u003Cdiv class=\"textpart\" lang=\"grc\">\u003Cspan class=\"ab\" id=\"div-diplomatic\">\u003Ca id=\"al1-diplomatic\">\u003C!--0-->\u003C/a>\u003Cspan class=\"linenumber\">1\u003C/span>\u003Cspan data-type=\"name\" data-name-type=\"\" data-text=\"Ἥρα\">ΗΡΑ\u003C/span>\u003C/span>\u003C/div>\u003Cp class=\"miniapp\">\u003C/p>\u003C/div>",{"id":168,"html":169},"facsimile-images","\u003Ch2>Facsimile Images\u003C/h2>",{"id":171,"html":172},"apparatus","\u003Ch4>Apparatus criticus\u003C/h4>\u003Cul>\u003Cli>\u003Cspan>\u003Cspan>Text after photograph\u003C/span>\u003C/span>\u003C/li>\u003C/ul>",{"id":174,"html":175},"translation","\u003Ch2>English translation\u003C/h2>\u003Cp class=\"translation-author\">\u003Ca href=\"https://orcid.org/0000-0002-7122-2511\">Valentina Mignosa\u003C/a>\u003C/p>  \u003Cp>(To?) Hera\u003C/p>  ",{"id":177,"html":178},"commentary","\u003Ch2>commentary\u003C/h2>  \u003Cp>The complete name of Hera is scratched on the exterior of the rim of a fragmentary black-glaze patera. Scerra regards the graffito as one of the few secure pieces of evidence for cult practice at Kamarina and notes that no earlier evidence for the worship of Hera had been identified in the city. Comparable vase graffiti giving the complete name of the goddess are known from Gela. The vessel type was current from the last quarter of the fifth century through much of the fourth century BCE, but the absence of a secure archaeological context prevents a more precise dating. However, the orthography of the inscription—with open long /ɛː/ represented by epsilon and aspiration by het—would favour an earlier date, between the mid-sixth and early fifth century BCE. Scerra considers that the graffito could record a devotional act performed by a Geloan settler after the refoundation of Kamarina in 461 BCE, while also suggesting that the cult may have reached the city earlier. The inscription supports the presence of devotion to Hera but does not by itself demonstrate the existence of a formal sanctuary. \u003C/p>  ",{"id":180,"html":181},"bibliography","\u003Ch2>bibliography\u003C/h2>  \u003Cul>\u003Cli> Scerra 2018 472-473, figs. 7-8 \u003Ca href=\"https://biblio.inscriptiones.org/epig10002691\" target=\"_blank\">https://biblio.inscriptiones.org/epig10002691\u003C/a>  \u003C/li>\u003C/ul>  \u003Cul>\u003Cli>\u003C/li>\u003C/ul>  ",{"id":183,"html":184},"handnote"," Line 1    ",{"id":186,"html":187},"license"," Licensed under a Creative Commons-Attribution 4.0 licence. ",[189,191,194,195],{"html":190},"Text encoding: Valentina Mignosa",{"id":192,"lang":100,"html":193},"edition-interpretive","\u003Cdiv class=\"textpart\" lang=\"grc\">\u003Cspan class=\"ab\" id=\"div-interpretive\">  \u003Ca id=\"al1-interpretive\">\u003C!--0-->\u003C/a>\u003Cspan class=\"linenumber\">1\u003C/span>\u003Cspan data-type=\"name\" data-name-type=\"\" data-text=\"Ἥρα\">Ἥρα\u003C/span>  \u003C/span>\u003C/div>  \u003Cp class=\"miniapp\">\u003C/p>",{"html":